==================================== INPUT ====================================
// Test: a Query-rooted resolver whose `@rootFragment` reads a plain server field
// is REJECTED under `@exec_time_resolvers`.
//
// This fixture used to assert the opposite — that such a resolver compiles and
// gets `hasServerField` metadata. It compiled only because the S2C identity-only
// rule carved `Query` out, on the grounds that a Query-rooted resolver reads only
// client state. One whose root fragment reads server data does not, and at exec
// time `hello` was projected from a fabricated record holding just `__typename`
// and `id`, so it read back as undefined with no error. The carve-out was hiding
// exactly the case the rule exists to catch.
//
// What still compiles is the magic fragment — see
// `exec_time_resolver_query_root_shadow_routes`, whose Query-rooted root fragment
// reaches server data through the `@__relay_shadow_return` corridor and keeps its
// `hasServerField` coverage.
//
// To make a shape like this legal again the field has to become projectable from
// the normalized payload, which is what the `@UNSTABLE_key` work adds. Until then this is
// a compile error rather than a silent undefined.
//- FooQuery.js
graphql`query FooQuery @exec_time_resolvers {
  greeting
}`

//- Query_greeting.js
/**
 * @relayField Query.greeting: RelayResolverValue
 * @rootFragment QueryGreetingFragment
 */
graphql`fragment QueryGreetingFragment on Query {
  hello
}`

//- relay.config.json
{
  "language": "flow",
  "jsModuleFormat": "haste",
  "schema": "schema.graphql",
  "featureFlags": {
    "enable_exec_time_resolvers_directive": true
  }
}

//- schema.graphql
type Query { hello: String }
==================================== OUTPUT ===================================
✖︎ Server-to-client resolver @rootFragment `QueryGreetingFragment` runs at exec time, where it is projected from the normalized response rather than read from the store. Found disallowed selection: `hello`. Allowed here: identity (`__typename`, `id`, `__id`), a field granted by `@UNSTABLE_key(fields: "...")` on the type that declares it, and the selections the resolver's own magic fragment transplants. Granting `hello` asserts it is stable for the record's lifetime and always arrives in the same payload as the record; where that does not hold, read the field at read time instead.

  Query_greeting.js:6:3
    5 │ fragment QueryGreetingFragment on Query {
    6 │   hello
      │   ^^^^^
    7 │ }
